Stack memory - 
	Stack memory is a memory area used by each thread to store method call information, including local variables, method parameters, and references to objects. Each method call creates a stack frame, which is removed when the method finishes execution.

What is a Stack Frame?
	Every time a method is called, Java creates a stack frame for that method.
	
What Goes Into Stack Memory?
	Local primitive variables, Method parameters, References to objects
	
other defintion - Stack memory is a per-thread memory area used to store method execution information. Each method call creates a stack frame containing local variables, parameters, and references. When the method returns, its stack frame is removed. Stack memory follows LIFO order, and excessive recursion can exhaust it and cause a StackOverflowError. Objects themselves are generally stored in heap memory, while local references to those objects are stored in stack frames.
	
