Single Responsibility Principle - 
	A class should have only one responsibility, and therefore it should have only one reason to change.
	
other definition - The Single Responsibility Principle states that a class should have one responsibility and therefore one reason to change. It encourages separating unrelated concerns into different classes, which improves maintainability, testability, and reduces coupling. It does not mean a class can have only one method; rather, its methods should be cohesive around a single responsibility.

